{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310579,"nonce":"OfBhVsyp5JPq/B/Z6lMYcVB0qOQ=","title":"MOSTLY RESOLVED by flash attention (commit 7835a26). The ~8 MB/token growth was almost entirely `expandKvHeads(K/V)` + score tensor churn ...","message":"On 808-token sustained Voronoi decode at commit 4001079 (spin pool):\n- **Peak resident: 690 MB** (user-measured)\n- Below the original llama.cpp footprint-class target of ~1-1.5 GB.\n- Likely contributors on top of flash-attn: spin pool (4001079) removed\n  `mpsc::channel` Send + node heap allocations across thousands of\n  fork-joins per token; topk NEON path (3730292) replaced per-call\n  FFI scratch with NEON registers; and the leak suspect ranking\n  pre-flash (KV expand bytes) was already gone.\n- Roughly: 640 MB Q4_K_M model mmap-resident + ~32 MB KV (f32) +\n  ~20 MB workspace/JIT = 692 MB. Matches the no-leak regime exactly.\n\n\n`rayzor_tensor_flash_attn_decode` (commit 7835a26) eliminated the\nexpand+score+context allocation pile in `GQAttention.forward` for the\ndecode case (seqQ=1). Same MZ-story 600-token workload:\n- Pre-flash peak resident: ~21 GB (user report this session arc)\n- Flash peak resident:     **~850 MB** — back in llama.cpp footprint\n  class (~1-1.5 GB for the same model + ctx).\n\nThe original framing (\"8 MB live growth per token, monotonic\") was\ncorrect on the symptom but slightly off on the cause: the growth was\nreal tensor data accumulating between collection points, not actual\nleaks. Per layer per token the unfused chain was producing:\n- `kAllExpanded` + `vAllExpanded`: 9.2 MB at cacheLen=568 (the 4× GQA\n  broadcast was the dominant cost — these grew linearly with cache)\n- 3 score tensors + 2 context views ≈ 240 KB\n\n× 16 layers ≈ 150 MB / decode token. With the pool's reuse + manual\n.free() calls in the Haxe modules, most was reclaimed before the next\nstep — but transient peak between tokens drove the 21 GB observation\non long contexts. Flash kills the source.\n\n**Residual question:** the original instrumentation also reported\n~250k allocations / token at ~38 bytes avg (small-object spam, not\ntensors). Flash removed the tensor bytes; whether the small-object\ncount is still 250k/token is unverified. Re-run TrackingAllocator if\nthat ever surfaces as a perf or memory issue. The infrastructure to\ndo so is now part of `rayzor debug run --alloc-stats` (commit 3914383).\n\n\n\nDiagnostic stack (all reverted at end of session — re-apply when iterating):\n- `TrackingAllocator` as `#[global_allocator]` in `src/main.rs`\n- Atomic counters in `runtime/src/lib.rs`: ALLOC_BYTES_TOTAL, FREE_BYTES_TOTAL, ALLOC_COUNT, FREE_COUNT, LIVE_BYTES_PEAK, BOX_COUNT\n- 13-bucket size histogram (≤8/16/32/64/128/256/512/1K/2K/4K/16K/64K/\u003e64K)\n- Periodic auto-dump every N allocs (`RAYZOR_ALLOC_DUMP_EVERY=N`)\n- Sampled PC-only graph (`RAYZOR_ALLOC_GRAPH=1`, `RAYZOR_ALLOC_GRAPH_RATE=N`) writing `/tmp/rayzor_alloc_graph.csv` with raw hex PCs — resolve offline via `atos -o BIN -arch arm64 0xPC`\n- atexit hook + SIGTRAP/SIGSEGV/SIGABRT signal handlers for crash-time dumps\n- `Cargo.toml [profile.release] debug = 1` for atos resolution of binary-text PCs\n\n\n| Generated tokens | Allocs | Frees | Free % | Alloc bytes | Free bytes | Live MB | Peak MB | Boxes |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 7 (\"Paris\", canonical) | 6.9M | 1.85M | 27% | 462 MB | 276 MB | **186** | 198 | 27 |\n| 24 (haiku) | 11.3M | 4.07M | 36% | 627 MB | 301 MB | **326** | 337 | 29 |\n| 27 (haiku EOS) | 12.1M | 4.46M | 37% | 657 MB | 305 MB | **351** | 362 | 29 |\n\n**Per-decode-step delta (measured during decode phase, ac=5M onwards):**\n- ~**250,000 allocations / token**\n- ~**8 MB live growth / token**\n- Only **15% free rate during decode** (allocs accumulate faster than they free)\n- Avg alloc size during decode: ~38 bytes (load phase was ~80)\n\nProjection consistent with the 30 GB OOM on \"linked list in rust\" (~3000 tokens): 8 MB × 3000 = 24 GB live + transient peak ≈ 30 GB resident. Math checks.\n\n\n| Bucket | Count | % of allocs |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| **≤8 bytes** | **4.5M** | **40%** |\n| ≤16 | 0.9M | 8% |\n| ≤32 | 1.96M | 17% |\n| **≤64** | **3.7M** | **33%** |\n| ≤128 | 29k | 0.3% |\n| ≤256 | 75k | 0.7% |\n| ≤512 | 40k | 0.4% |\n| ≤1K | 31k | 0.3% |\n| ≤2K | 67k | 0.6% |\n| ≤4K | 10k | \u003c0.1% |\n| ≤16K | 5.7k | \u003c0.1% |\n| ≤64K | 499 | tiny |\n| \u003e64K | 118 | tiny |\n\n**73% of all allocations are ≤64 bytes.** Signature of small-object spam, not tensor allocations (those are MB-sized in the \u003e64K bucket).\n\n\n1. **Primitive boxing** (`haxe_box_int_ptr` / `_float_ptr` / `_bool_ptr` / `_cstring_ptr` / `_haxestring_ptr`). Added direct counter: **27 calls total** for 7-token run, **29 for 24-token run**. Per-step delta ≈ 0. Box helpers are NOT the source.\n2. **Tensor allocation buffers**. The tensor pool reuses wrappers + data, and tensor sizes are MB-class (would appear in \u003e64K bucket, where we see only 118 allocations). The leak is small-object dominated.\n\n\nSampled the call stack at 1/2048 of allocs via `backtrace::trace` (raw PCs only, no symbol resolution at sample time — too slow). Top 10 sites by sampled count:\n\n- **Rank 1**: 3207 samples (~6.6M est. allocs), first PC = `0x1021bf998` — in JIT region.\n- **Ranks 2-3**: ~880 + 470 samples each, PCs span `0x1021xxxxx` → `0x1024xxxxx` → `0x103axxxxx` → `0x103bxxxxx`. All JIT.\n- **Ranks 5-8**: shallow stacks (only PC1 set) → JIT-direct allocators. PCs `0x10358xxxx` / `0x10359xxxx`. All JIT.\n- **Rank 11**: `0x100f244e8` etc. — these are in main binary (0x100xxxxxx range) but `atos -debug=1` only resolved them to nearest *exported* symbol (zstd / wasmtime / wgpu internals) — useless without `debug = 2`.\n\n**73%+ of leak attribution points to JITed Haxe code**, which has no symbols accessible to `atos`. The 27% in the rayzor binary itself was too coarse to act on with debug=1.\n\n\n1. **JIT PCs have no symbols**. Cranelift emits machine code to mmap'd RWX pages with no entry in the binary's symbol table. `atos` and `addr2line` cannot resolve them.\n2. **Binary symbols need full debug info**. `debug = 1` (line tables only) gives `atos` only the nearest exported symbol, which is misleading for static functions and inlined code. `debug = 2` gives proper resolution but the binary balloons (~1+ GB likely) and rebuilds are slow.\n\n\n\nCranelift's `JITModule::get_finalized_function(FuncId)` returns the entry address of each compiled function. After all functions finalize, walk the module and build a `Vec\u003c(FuncId, start_addr, end_addr, name)\u003e` table. Persist to `/tmp/rayzor_jit_symbols.txt`. At PC resolution time (offline tool), look up which (FuncId, name) owns each PC.\n\nPlumbing:\n- Add a hook in `compiler/src/codegen/cranelift_backend.rs` post-finalize that iterates `JITModule.declarations()` and dumps `addr,size,name` per fn\n- Add an `RAYZOR_DUMP_JIT_MAP=1` env var\n- Write a small `tools/resolve_alloc_graph.py` that joins `/tmp/rayzor_alloc_graph.csv` against `/tmp/rayzor_jit_symbols.txt`\n\nEstimated: ~200 LOC + small Python script. One-time cost; usable across all future leak investigations.\n\n\nModify `nue/sampling/GenerationLoop.hx` to disable specific operations per step. Measure leak delta after each cut:\n1. Skip `tokenizer.decode(generated)` — does `model.forwardIds([nextId])` alone leak? Likely YES (forward pass is the bulk).\n2. Skip `sampler.sample(lastRow(logits))` — does the per-step ops minus sampler still leak? Filters out sampler.\n3. In `model.forwardIds`, comment out the FFN block in `TransformerBlock.forward` — does attention alone leak?\n4. In `GQAttention.forward`, comment out KV cache append — does the rest leak?\n\nEach cut requires a `.rayzor` scrub + rebuild. Net wall: ~30 minutes for 4 cuts.\n\nExpected outcome: the deepest cut that DROPS the per-step delta from 8 MB to near-zero identifies the leaky subsystem. Then look at its Haxe source for the construct that bypasses `InsertFreePass`.\n\n**Recommended:** Path B first (cheaper, often pinpoints in one round). Path A if Path B narrows to \"the per-block forward loop\" and we need finer attribution.\n\n\n```rust\n// runtime/src/lib.rs — paste after the `const TRACKED_MAX_SIZE` line:\nuse std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering as MemOrdering};\npub static ALLOC_BYTES_TOTAL: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64::new(0);\npub static FREE_BYTES_TOTAL: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64::new(0);\npub static ALLOC_COUNT: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64::new(0);\npub static FREE_COUNT: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64::new(0);\npub static LIVE_BYTES_PEAK: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64::new(0);\n\npub struct TrackingAllocator;\nunsafe impl std::alloc::GlobalAlloc for TrackingAllocator {\n    unsafe fn alloc(\u0026self, layout: std::alloc::Layout) -\u003e *mut u8 {\n        let p = std::alloc::System.alloc(layout);\n        if !p.is_null() {\n            let sz = layout.size() as u64;\n            let prev = ALLOC_BYTES_TOTAL.fetch_add(sz, MemOrdering::Relaxed);\n            ALLOC_COUNT.fetch_add(1, MemOrdering::Relaxed);\n            let live = (prev + sz).saturating_sub(FREE_BYTES_TOTAL.load(MemOrdering::Relaxed));\n            LIVE_BYTES_PEAK.fetch_max(live, MemOrdering::Relaxed);\n        }\n        p\n    }\n    unsafe fn dealloc(\u0026self, ptr: *mut u8, layout: std::alloc::Layout) {\n        std::alloc::System.dealloc(ptr, layout);\n        FREE_BYTES_TOTAL.fetch_add(layout.size() as u64, MemOrdering::Relaxed);\n        FREE_COUNT.fetch_add(1, MemOrdering::Relaxed);\n    }\n}\n\npub extern \"C\" fn rayzor_dump_alloc_stats() {\n    let a = ALLOC_BYTES_TOTAL.load(MemOrdering::Relaxed);\n    let f = FREE_BYTES_TOTAL.load(MemOrdering::Relaxed);\n    eprintln!(\"[alloc-stats] allocs={} frees={} alloc_bytes={} free_bytes={} live={} peak={}\",\n        ALLOC_COUNT.load(MemOrdering::Relaxed),\n        FREE_COUNT.load(MemOrdering::Relaxed),\n        a, f, a.saturating_sub(f), LIVE_BYTES_PEAK.load(MemOrdering::Relaxed));\n}\n```\n\n```rust\n// src/main.rs — paste at top of main():\nstatic GLOBAL: rayzor_runtime::TrackingAllocator = rayzor_runtime::TrackingAllocator;\nfn main() {\n    extern \"C\" { fn atexit(cb: extern \"C\" fn()) -\u003e i32; }\n    unsafe { atexit(rayzor_runtime::rayzor_dump_alloc_stats); }\n    // ... rest of main\n}\n```\n\nThen: `cargo build --release` (4 min cold). Run:\n```bash\n/path/to/rayzor run llama-chat/Main.hx -- \u003cgguf\u003e \"PROMPT\" N 4096 0.01 2\u003e\u00261 | grep '\\[alloc-stats\\]'\n```\n\nThe histogram + PC graph code is more elaborate — see this session's transcript at the path stored in `originSessionId` if you need to replay them verbatim.\n\n\n- The leak measurement above runs with the global allocator wrapped. Hot path overhead: ~5 relaxed atomic ops per alloc/dealloc. Measured baseline ~5-6 tok/s vs baseline ~15 tok/s WITHOUT the wrapper. **Disable counters before any perf benchmark.**\n- `Cargo.toml [profile.release] debug = 1` triggers a full rebuild. Remove when not actively profiling — it doubles the binary size.\n- `RAYZOR_ALLOC_GRAPH_RATE=2048` is a reasonable starting point. Lower rates (256) deadlocked previously due to symbol-resolution-at-sample-time. Current code uses raw-PC capture and resolves offline — safer to go lower if needed.\n\n\n- [[bugs-mark-escaping-incomplete]] — escape-analysis fix template; if Path B pins the leak to a Haxe construct missing from `InsertFreePass`, the fix likely mirrors this.\n- [[bugs-anon-setter-value-escape]] — value-arg escape exemption; another instance of the same bug class.\n- [[bugs-stringmap-null-get]] — REFUTED for this leak (boxes are only 27 total) but real bug remaining for future cleanup.\n- [[project-optimization-roadmap]] — this is Tier 1 #1.\n- [[project-llama-chat-coherence-phase-c]] — bmm stride-awareness; possibly related if the leak lives in attention.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_decode_loop_per_step_leak` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}